From: substrate
Subject: dix: MakeAtom rejects NULL/low-junk string

Kernel TRAP backtrace caught Xfbdev SIGSEGVing in MakeAtom at
dix/atom.c:85 (`fp = fp * 27 + string[i]`) with addr=0x0 — caller
is libXfont's bdfForceMakeAtom(charName, NULL) at bdfread.c:355
parsing a misc-fixed BDF, where `charName` came out of:

    if (sscanf((char *)line, "STARTCHAR %99s", charName) != 1) ...
    bitmapExtra->glyphNames[ndx] = bdfForceMakeAtom(charName, NULL);

charName is a stack buffer that's only written if sscanf returned 1.
Something in the substrate sscanf path appears to be reporting 1
without filling the buffer (or with a corrupted pointer);
investigation continues, but the MakeAtom side can defend itself:
return None for any pointer below 0x10000.

Visible artifact = no atom registered for that glyph name (it'll
appear unnamed in fontinfo), beats a SIGSEGV killing the font load
and crashing the X server.

--- a/dix/atom.c
+++ b/dix/atom.c
@@ -76,6 +76,18 @@ MakeAtom(const char *string, unsigned len, Bool makeit)
     unsigned int fp = 0;

+    /* Substrate hardening: a caller passing a NULL or low-junk
+     * pointer (observed from libXfont's bdfForceMakeAtom when
+     * sscanf doesn't fill the charName buffer) drives this loop
+     * into a NULL read at string[i].  Refuse the lookup and let
+     * the caller continue with None — visible "missing atom"
+     * artifact beats a SIGSEGV during font init. */
+    if ((uintptr_t)string < 0x10000) {
+        ErrorF("MakeAtom: refusing junk string=%p len=%u\n",
+               (const void *)string, len);
+        return None;
+    }
+
     np = &atomRoot;
     for (i = 0; i < (len + 1) / 2; i++) {
         fp = fp * 27 + string[i];
EOF_PATCH
